---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy Policy — WakeSharp"
  description="WakeSharp has no accounts and no ads. Core alarm, score and calendar data stay on your device; optional analytics and referrals use bounded anonymous records."
  path="/privacy"
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  intro="WakeSharp is an alarm clock that runs on your phone. It has no accounts and no advertising. Your core alarm, score and calendar data stay on your device; the bounded data used for optional analytics, attribution, purchases and referrals is listed on this page, exactly."
>
  <div class="callout">
    <h2 class="!mt-0">The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>There is no WakeSharp account. We never ask for your name, email or phone number.</li>
      <li>Your alarms, missions, game results, Sharpness scores and streaks are stored <strong>only on your device</strong>.</li>
      <li>Calendar access is <strong>optional and read-only</strong>. Your events are read on your phone to work out a wake time and are <strong>never transmitted anywhere</strong>.</li>
      <li>The <strong>camera</strong> (scan missions) and your <strong>step count</strong> (walking missions) are optional, used only while that mission is running, and processed <strong>entirely on your device</strong>. No image or step data ever leaves your phone.</li>
      <li><strong>Anonymous usage analytics</strong> describe which features are used and where people get stuck. You can turn them off. They never contain alarm labels or calendar content.</li>
      <li>If you explicitly join referrals, WakeSharp also sends a random installation credential, a referral code or claim, and signed qualifying-day assertions. The optional service is described below and can be deleted independently of your on-device mornings.</li>
      <li><strong>Purchase data</strong> goes to Apple or Google and RevenueCat if you buy or receive WakeSharp Plus.</li>
      <li>No advertising ID. No location. No tracking across other apps or websites, and nothing is ever sold.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    WakeSharp is published by {SITE.publisher}, a small independent studio. For anything in this policy,
    write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. For data-protection purposes, that is
    also the controller for the limited processing described below.
  </p>

  <h2>What WakeSharp does not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It does not create an account or a user profile.</li>
    <li>It does not collect your name, email address, phone number or contacts.</li>
    <li>It does not request or use your location.</li>
    <li>It does not use an advertising identifier, and it shows no ads.</li>
    <li>It does not track you across other apps or websites. The usage analytics described below measure WakeSharp itself, are keyed to a random per-install ID, and are never combined with outside data or used for advertising.</li>
    <li>It does not sell or share personal information, in the sense those words are given by the CCPA/CPRA.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
  <p>
    WakeSharp's detailed morning data is written to a local database on your phone
    (SwiftData on iOS, Room on Android). It is not uploaded. If you explicitly use referrals, the server
    receives only a signed assertion that a qualifying day occurred, not the detailed morning record:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your alarms, their times, repeat days and settings.</li>
    <li>Your smart-alarm rules and the wake times computed from them.</li>
    <li>A journal of alarms scheduled, fired, snoozed, missed or silenced by a completed mission.</li>
    <li>Your warm-up game results, personal baseline, daily Sharpness scores, streaks and freeze tokens. A referral assertion says only that full credit occurred on a local day within the allowed time.</li>
    <li>Your app preferences.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On Android the app declares <code>android:allowBackup="false"</code>, so this data is not copied into
    Android's cloud backup. Deleting the app removes all of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Calendar access — optional, read-only, and it never leaves your phone</h2>
  <p>
    WakeSharp can wake you a set number of minutes before your first meeting. To do that it needs to read
    your calendar. This is how that works:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>It is <strong>entirely optional</strong>. Decline it and every other feature still works — you just set your alarm times yourself.</li>
    <li>Access is <strong>read-only</strong>. WakeSharp never creates, edits or deletes an event.</li>
    <li>Events are read <strong>on your device</strong>, and used for one purpose: to compute the time your alarm should ring.</li>
    <li>Event titles, attendees, locations, notes and times are <strong>never transmitted to us or to anyone else</strong>. They are not written to any server, and they are not included in any diagnostic report.</li>
    <li>You can revoke calendar access at any time in your phone's system settings. The smart alarm stops adjusting; nothing else changes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Permissions, and why each one exists</h2>
  <p>On iPhone, WakeSharp asks for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Alarms</strong> — so WakeSharp can present scheduled system alarms through AlarmKit, including supported Silent mode and Focus behavior.</li>
    <li><strong>Calendar</strong> — optional, read-only, for smart alarms only.</li>
    <li><strong>Camera</strong> — optional, only for an alarm you have set to a scan mission, and only while that mission is running. See the next section.</li>
    <li><strong>Motion &amp; Fitness</strong> — optional, only for an alarm you have set to a walking mission, to count the steps it asks for. See the next section.</li>
    <li><strong>Notifications</strong> — for the optional pre-bed wind-down reminder, the reliability warning that rides with it, and the report shown when an alarm did not ring.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>On Android, the app declares exactly these permissions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>INTERNET</code> — for the subscription and analytics services described below. No alarm needs it.</li>
    <li><code>USE_EXACT_ALARM</code> — to schedule an alarm that fires at the exact minute you asked for.</li>
    <li><code>SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM</code> — the same thing on Android 12 and 12L only, which predate the permission above. It is dropped from the build on Android 13 and later.</li>
    <li><code>USE_FULL_SCREEN_INTENT</code> — to show the ringing screen over the lock screen.</li>
    <li><code>POST_NOTIFICATIONS</code> — for the ring notification, the optional bedtime reminder, and the missed-alarm report.</li>
    <li><code>FOREGROUND_SERVICE</code> and <code>FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PLAYBACK</code> — to keep the alarm tone playing while the alarm is ringing.</li>
    <li><code>RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED</code> — to restore your alarms after a restart, because Android discards scheduled alarms on reboot.</li>
    <li><code>READ_CALENDAR</code> — optional, read-only, for smart alarms only.</li>
    <li><code>CAMERA</code> — optional, for scan missions only. See the next section.</li>
    <li><code>ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION</code> — optional, for walking missions only. See the next section.</li>
    <li><code>WAKE_LOCK</code> and <code>VIBRATE</code> — to wake the screen and vibrate while ringing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The camera, its autofocus and the step counter are all declared <strong>not required</strong>, so
    WakeSharp still installs on a phone that has none of them — the missions that need them simply are not
    offered there.
  </p>

  <h2>The camera and your physical activity</h2>
  <p>
    Two of the missions read a sensor. Both are optional, both ask for permission when you set the alarm
    rather than at 6am, and neither sends anything anywhere.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The scan mission</strong> asks you to point the camera at one of a fixed list of everyday
      objects. Recognition runs <strong>entirely on your device</strong> — Apple's Vision framework on
      iPhone, a small model bundled with the app on Android. Frames are analysed in memory and discarded.
      Nothing is photographed, written to storage, added to your photo library, or transmitted to us or to
      anyone else. Only the names of the objects an alarm asks for are saved, on your phone, like any other
      alarm setting.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The walking mission</strong> reads your device's own step counter, and only while that mission
      is running, to check that the steps it asked for actually happened. It is a count, not a route:
      WakeSharp never requests or receives your location.
    </li>
    <li>
      Decline either permission and nothing breaks. The mission falls back to a math sprint at full credit,
      rather than leaving you with an alarm you cannot silence.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Purchases and WakeSharp Plus</h2>
  <p>
    Every alarm and every mission in WakeSharp is free, forever. WakeSharp Plus is optional: a subscription
    at {SITE.plus.monthly}/month or {SITE.plus.annual}/year, or a one-time {SITE.plus.lifetime} Lifetime
    purchase that never renews. It adds three rotating warm-up games each morning, scan missions on every
    alarm, your full Sharpness history, unlimited smart calendar alarms, and the Lark scenes, wallpapers
    and celebrations.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you buy it, the purchase itself is handled entirely by <strong>Apple</strong> or{' '}
    <strong>Google</strong>. We never see your card, your billing address, or your Apple or Google account
    details.
  </p>
  <p>
    To know whether Plus is unlocked, WakeSharp uses <strong>RevenueCat, Inc.</strong>, a
    subscription-management service. When you open the paywall or restore a purchase, the RevenueCat SDK
    sends:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What is sent</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>A randomly generated, anonymous app user ID</td><td>To tie a receipt to an install. It is not derived from your name, email or any device identifier you can be traced by.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The store receipt / purchase token from Apple or Google</td><td>To validate the purchase and decide whether Plus is unlocked.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>IP address, and the country derived from it or from your store account</td><td>Sent with every network request; used for fraud checks and to price and report by region.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Device model, operating-system version and app version</td><td>Sent by the SDK with each request, and used to diagnose purchase problems.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    RevenueCat processes this in the <strong>United States</strong>, as our processor and under their data
    processing terms, including standard contractual clauses where they are required for transfers from the
    UK or the EEA. Their own privacy policy is at{' '}
    <a href="https://www.revenuecat.com/privacy" rel="noopener">revenuecat.com/privacy</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    RevenueCat also shows us <strong>anonymous, aggregate</strong> subscription numbers — how many trials
    started, how many subscriptions are active. We cannot identify you from it. RevenueCat additionally
    forwards subscription events (a trial started, a renewal, a cancellation) to our analytics project
    described in the next section, keyed to the same random analytics ID — so we can see, in aggregate,
    how free users become subscribers.
  </p>

  <h2>Usage analytics and session replay (PostHog)</h2>
  <p>
    WakeSharp uses <strong>PostHog, Inc.</strong>, a product-analytics service, to understand how the app
    is used — which screens people visit, where onboarding is abandoned, whether the paywall is confusing.
    This is the only analytics SDK in the app, and it works without knowing who you are:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What is sent</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>A randomly generated, per-install analytics ID</td><td>To count you once. It is created on your device, is not derived from your name, email or any identifier you can be traced by, and resets if you reinstall the app.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Usage events — screens viewed, features used (an alarm was created, a mission was completed, the paywall was shown), bounded acquisition fields, your onboarding survey answers, and whether Plus is active</td><td>To measure what works and improve the app. Events describe <em>that</em> something happened, never its content: not your alarm times or labels, not your calendar events, not referral credentials, and not your signature.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Masked session recordings — a replay of screen layouts where <strong>all text and images are obscured</strong> before anything leaves your device</td><td>To see where people get stuck. What we can watch back is the shape of the interaction, not what was on your screen.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Device model, operating-system version, app version, language, and the approximate region PostHog derives from the request's IP address</td><td>To diagnose device-specific problems and see roughly where users are. We see country-level information, not your location.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    PostHog processes this in the <strong>United States</strong>, as our processor and under their data
    processing agreement, including standard contractual clauses where required for transfers from the UK
    or the EEA. Their privacy policy is at{' '}
    <a href="https://posthog.com/privacy" rel="noopener">posthog.com/privacy</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no advertising involved: this data is never combined with third-party data, never used to
    target you, and never sold.
  </p>

  <h2>First-party acquisition measurement</h2>
  <p>
    WakeSharp measures where an install came from without an advertising identifier, an ATT prompt,
    fingerprinting, cross-app tracking or automatic clipboard reading. Android can read Google Play's
    Install Referrer once and keeps only normalized source, channel, campaign, ad-group and creative
    fields; the raw referrer is discarded. On iPhone, Apple Search Ads attribution is read through
    AdServices without storing or logging Apple's token. Other eligible iPhone campaigns are reported by
    Apple's aggregate AdAttributionKit postbacks. Those postbacks are not joined to a person or installation.
  </p>

  <h2>Optional referrals</h2>
  <p>
    Referrals are off by default and do not affect whether an alarm rings. If you choose to create or claim
    a referral, WakeSharp registers an anonymous installation public key after Apple App Attest or Google
    Play Integrity validation. The attestation payload and raw Play referrer are discarded after validation.
    The service stores the public key, a one-way hash of a random install credential, an anonymous
    RevenueCat app-user ID, the referral code and claim, qualifying local-day assertions, reward status and
    a bounded audit trail. It never receives alarm labels, times, calendar contents, meeting details,
    Sharpness results or sensor data.
  </p>
  <p>
    A signed qualifying-day assertion contains a random event ID, local day, timestamp, time-zone offset and
    the number of minutes taken to complete the mission. It must represent full credit from a real wake alarm.
    Duplicate days, replayed requests, self-referrals, expired claims and failed integrity checks are rejected.
    RevenueCat receives the existing anonymous app-user ID and the entitlement expiry needed to fulfill an
    approved reward. Lifetime users receive recognition only; no redundant entitlement is created.
  </p>
  <p>
    Referral records are held in an encrypted-in-transit Neon Postgres database in the United States.
    Normalized records expire 180 days after final activity. You can delete the installation credential and
    directly associated assertions from WakeSharp Settings; a minimal anonymized audit record remains only
    until the same 180-day limit. Neon's privacy policy is at{' '}
    <a href="https://neon.com/privacy-policy" rel="noopener">neon.com/privacy-policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact-form">The contact form on this website</h2>
  <p>
    This section is about <a href="/contact">wakesharp.app/contact</a> only. The app itself never sends a
    message anywhere, and everything above still holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you use that form, the name, email address, topic, optional device details and message you type are
    delivered to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> as an ordinary email by{' '}
    <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy" rel="noopener">Resend</a>, the service that carries
    it. Submitting also triggers a single automatic confirmation email to the address you entered; its
    text is fixed and contains nothing from your submission. There is no database behind the form — nothing is written to disk on our side, and your submission
    exists only as the resulting email in our inbox. It is kept while the correspondence is useful and then
    deleted; Resend holds a short-lived delivery log under its own retention policy. Because it is ordinary
    email and not a secure channel, please do not send anything sensitive through it.
  </p>

  <h2>How long data is kept, and how to delete it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>On-device data</strong> — kept until you delete it in the app or uninstall WakeSharp. Uninstalling removes it. Referral assertions, if you opted in, are separate and can be deleted from Settings before uninstalling.</li>
    <li><strong>Referral records</strong> — expire 180 days after final activity. An authenticated deletion request immediately revokes and anonymizes the installation credential, removes successful-day assertions and invalidates its referral link.</li>
    <li><strong>Purchase records at RevenueCat</strong> — kept while the record exists, under RevenueCat's own retention policy.</li>
    <li><strong>Analytics data at PostHog</strong> — usage events are kept for as long as we analyse them; session recordings are kept for a shorter period under PostHog's retention settings. To have your analytics record deleted, email us — if you can send it from the app's device or tell us roughly when you used the app and on what device model, we can locate the random ID and delete everything attached to it.</li>
    <li>
      Because both the subscription ID and the analytics ID are anonymous, neither you nor we can identify
      your record from the app itself. For the subscription record, email us from the address on your Apple
      or Google receipt and include the purchase date — we will use the store transaction to locate and
      delete it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Cancelling a subscription is separate and is done in your App Store or Google Play account. Deleting the app does not cancel a subscription. A Lifetime purchase is not a subscription and has nothing to cancel.</p>

  <h2>Children's privacy</h2>
  <p>
    WakeSharp is rated 4+ / Everyone and is suitable for all ages, but it is not directed at children. It
    does not request names, email addresses, phone numbers or precise location. The limited anonymous
    technical records described above apply in the same way regardless of age.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Depending on where you live, the GDPR, the UK GDPR, the CCPA/CPRA and similar laws give you rights to
    access, correct, delete, port or object to the processing of your personal data.
  </p>
  <p>
    In practice, we hold no name or contact identity for an app installation. Your on-device data is already
    in your hands — you can see it in the app and destroy it by uninstalling. Anonymous subscription,
    analytics and optional referral records use the deletion routes described above.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where we do process data, our legal basis is the performance of our contract with you (validating a
    subscription you bought), and our legitimate interest in understanding how the app is used and in
    preventing payment fraud. You have the right to complain to your local data-protection authority.
  </p>

  <h2>Security</h2>
  <p>
    Your detailed morning data stays inside your phone's app sandbox and is protected by your device's own
    encryption and passcode. Traffic to RevenueCat, PostHog and the optional referral service uses HTTPS.
    Referral API calls use an installation credential plus a device-held signing key, replay protection and
    integrity validation. No system is perfectly secure, so referral rewards remain independent of essential
    alarm behavior.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes we will update the date at the top of this page. Material changes will be
    described here rather than made quietly.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> — {SITE.publisher}, publisher of WakeSharp.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
